  with custom type tagging — which is exactly the kind of machinery that becomes load-bearing and then wrong.

  Two values get special handling on write:
  - [38;5;153mset(key, undefined)[39m (also a function or a symbol) — [38;5;153mJSON.stringify[39m yields no string at all for these. Rather than
    write the literal text [38;5;153m"undefined"[39m and hand it back as a string forever, this is treated as [38;5;153mremove(key)[39m and returns
    [38;5;153mtrue[39m. Deleting a preference by setting it to [38;5;153mundefined[39m is the behavior people expect.
  - A cyclic object, or a [38;5;153mBigInt[39m — [38;5;153mJSON.stringify[39m throws. [38;5;153mset[39m catches it, writes nothing, and returns [38;5;153mfalse[39m. A bad value
    from one caller never corrupts the store or escapes as an exception into a click handler.

  [1mCorrupt reads.[0m If [38;5;153mJSON.parse[39m throws — hand-edited storage, a write truncated by a crash — [38;5;153mget[39m returns the fallback. It
  does [1mnot[0m delete the bad entry. A read that silently destroys data is a worse surprise than a read that returns a
  default, and keeping the value means it's still there to inspect when someone asks why a setting reset.

  [1mWhen [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m isn't available.[0m Two distinct failures, both real:
  - [3mUnavailable at startup[0m — Safari private browsing, blocked site data, [38;5;153mfile://[39m sandboxing. The factory probes once by
    writing and removing a namespaced probe key inside a [38;5;153mtry[39m. If that throws, or [38;5;153mstorage[39m is missing entirely, the
    instance routes everything to an in-memory [38;5;153mMap[39m and sets [38;5;153misPersistent = false[39m. The app works; settings just don't
    survive the tab.
  - [3mFailing later[0m — quota exhaustion hits on a specific [38;5;153mset[39m, long after a successful probe. That [38;5;153mset[39m catches the throw,
    keeps the value in an in-memory overlay, and returns [38;5;153mfalse[39m. [38;5;153mget[39m checks the overlay before [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m, so a failed
    durable write is never shadowed by a stale persisted value underneath it, and [38;5;153mremove[39m clears both.

  So [38;5;153mset[39m's boolean return is meaningful in both cases, and [38;5;153misPersistent[39m tells a caller up front whether to bother
  showing a "settings won't be saved" notice.

  [1mOn the security boundary.[0m I want to be precise rather than reassuring: [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m is readable by any script running
  on the page and by anyone with access to the machine. It is not a secret store, and no design here changes that. The
  protection is that the login form stays unwired from this module and no username, password, token, or "remember me"
  flag goes through it. I'm not adding key-name filtering to block credentials — a blocklist of names like [38;5;153mpassword[39m
  would catch nothing a real mistake would use, and would mostly serve to make the store [3mfeel[0m safe. The contract gets
  stated in the module's header comment instead.

  Does the storage and failure design look right before I cover integration and testing?

